  "final_text": "I finished all four steps, and the rule was still enabled when I read it back at the end.\n\n**1. Data view.** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`, index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has 45 fields, and its time field is `timestamp`. The index also has an `@timestamp` field, but the data view doesn't use it. The main fields are:\n- **Dates:** `timestamp`, `@timestamp`, `utc_time`\n- **Request text fields, each with a `.keyword` version:** `response`, `request`, `url`, `agent`, `host`, `extension`, `machine.os`, `message`, `tags`\n- **Numbers:** `bytes`, `memory`, `machine.ram`, `phpmemory`, `hour_of_day`\n- **Network and location:** `clientip`, `ip`, `geo.coordinates`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `referer`\n\nThe response code is stored as text (`response.keyword`), not as a number.\n\n**2. Dashboard.** I created \"S4-63e39fcd Web logs\" (id `690538f8-9dd1-436b-a974-a91249e5f9cf`) with exactly two panels, both using that data view:\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart counting requests per time interval on `timestamp`.\n- **Requests by response code:** a bar chart counting requests for each value of `response.keyword`, showing up to 10 codes.\n\n**3. Alert rule.** I created \"S4-63e39fcd 5xx errors\" (id `0b15f3da-02da-4f88-808a-2acbed729b13`) on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 server errors arrive within 5 minutes. Because the response code is text, a simple number range wouldn't work reliably. Instead the rule matches every code from 500 to 599 by pattern (`5[0-9][0-9]`). The rule was created enabled, so it didn't need a separate enable step.\n\n**4. Edit and check.** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\", and the saved dashboard shows the new title. The rule, read back afterwards, reports enabled = true.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
